URL initially, did we get any cookies?\n# Let's look at the response headers of the first request:\n# Set-Cookie: nevercache-b39818=Y;Max-Age=-1\n# No other cookies.\n# But wait! Let's look at the request headers that the python session sent to the solution URL!\n# Wait! Does the python session send any other headers?\n# What if SiteGround's WAF checks the `Sec-Ch-Ua` headers or other client hints?\n# Or what if SiteGround is blocking Python's urllib/requests altogether, even with a modified User-Agent?\n# Let's think: how can we verify if the 403 Forbidden is because of the cookie, or because Python requests is being detected?\n# Wait! Let's check if we get a 403 Forbidden on `https://coinography.com/` if we make a request with Python requests but *without* any cookie, but with the same User-Agent?\n# No, without the cookie we got a 202 (not a 403!).\n# This is a critical observation!\n# Without the cookie -> 202 (Challenge page)\n# With the cookie -> 403 Forbidden!\n# Why would a request *with* the correct cookie return 403 Forbidden, while the same request *without* the cookie returns 202?\n# Let's think!\n# Could it be because the cookie we are sending has some mismatch with the WAF's expectations?\n# Let's look at the cookie value:\n# `f37141aa9a6f05286d05f96eb6dd6b6927f15cd21a4c06b34219d4180e820576-1781675303-21`\n# Wait, look at the suffix of the cookie value: `-21`.\n# 21 is the complexity!\n# Look at the timestamp in the middle of the cookie value: `1781675303`.\n# That is exactly the epoch timestamp of when the captcha was solved or generated.\n# But wait, look at the first part: `f37141aa9a6f05286d05f96eb6dd6b6927f15cd21a4c06b34219d4180e820576`.\n# This is a SHA-256 hash.\n# Wait! How is that SHA-256 hash generated by SiteGround?\n# It must be a hash of the IP address, timestamp, complexity, and a server-side secret.\n# If the SHA-256 hash was generated by the server and sent to us in the `Set-Cookie` header of the solution response, then the cookie value