us to access any page on the site.\n# Wait! Let's check if the cookie needs to have the secure attribute, or if the domain needs to match exactly?\n# Let's look at how the browser would send the cookie.\n# In the browser, the cookie domain is `.coinography.com`.\n# The path is `/`.\n# What if we send the cookie as a header `Cookie: _I_=...` manually in python requests?\n# Yes, we did that.\n# Let's think: is there any other header that the browser sends, but Python requests doesn't, that causes the WAF to reject the cookie?\n# What about the `Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate, br`?\n# Python requests supports `gzip` and `deflate`, but does it support `br` (Brotli)?\n# Yes, if `brotli` package is installed. But if not, requests might send headers that don't match what SiteGround expects for a modern browser.\n# Wait! Let's check what headers `sg_solver.py` sent when it requested `https://coinography.com/` and triggered the redirect:\n# It didn't send any custom headers other than `User-Agent`.\n# But wait! Let's look at the response of the submission request again:\n# `Solution response status: 202`\n# Wait, why was the status code of the submission request 202?\n# Yes, SiteGround returns 202 Accepted for the solution submission, which contains the Javascript to set the cookie and redirect.\n# But wait! Does the submission request itself need to set the cookie?\n# Let's look at the JS code in the submission response:\n# `document.cookie=\"_I_=...;max-age=2592000;path=/;domain=\" +l.host.replace(/^www./, '');`\n# Wait! The JS code sets the cookie in the browser!\n# But did the HTTP response of the submission request *also* have a `Set-Cookie` header?\n# Yes:\n# `'Set-Cookie': '_I_=f37141aa9a6f05286d05f96eb6dd6b6927f15cd21a4c06b34219d4180e820576-1781675303-21; expires=Fri, 17-Jul-26 05:48:23 GMT; max-age=2592000; path=/; domain=.coinography.com; HttpOnly, nevercache-b39818=Y;Max-Age=-1'`\n# So both the `Set-Cookie` header and the JS set the cookie.\n# But wait! Is there a