{"type":"message","id":"f3eb6bf5","parentId":"03f55270","timestamp":"2026-06-17T06:44:29.476Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**HTTP** (with two \"t\"s, sometimes mistyped as **htttp**) stands for **Hypertext Transfer Protocol**. It is the standard language that web browsers, servers, and scripts use to talk to each other.\n\nWhenever our script communicates with your WordPress site, the server replies with an **HTTP Status Code** to tell us how the request went:\n* **HTTP 200 (Success):** \"Everything went perfectly, here is your data!\" (e.g., Rank Math update).\n* **HTTP 201 (Created):** \"Success! I have created the new post / uploaded the image!\" (e.g., WordPress post creation).\n* **HTTP 202 (Accepted / Challenge):** \"I received the request, but you must pass a security check first.\" (This is where SiteGround's Captcha challenge popped up).\n* **HTTP 401 (Unauthorized):** \"You reached WordPress, but you are not logged in yet.\"\n* **HTTP 403 (Forbidden):** \"Access denied. The firewall blocked the request.\"\n\n### Why it is mentioned so much\nBecause Scribe is an automated publishing agent, it relies entirely on these **HTTP Status Codes** to know if each step of the pipeline was successful. If the server returns an error code (like `403` or `202`), the script immediately logs it so we can troubleshoot and bypass the issue.\n\n*Note: If you saw **\"htttp\"** with three 't's, that was simply a typo of **HTTP** (or HTTPS)!*"}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gemini-3.5-flash","usage":{"input":94265,"output":814,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":95079,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1781678662640,"responseId":"SEIyaoSTPNyp8uMP9brgIQ"}}
{"type":"message","id":"8f07949d","parentId":"f3eb6bf5","timestamp":"2026-06-17T06:44:55.236Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sender (untrusted metadata):\n```json\n{\n  \"label\": \"openclaw-control-ui\",\n  \"id\": \"openclaw-control-ui\"\n}\n```\n\n[Wed 2026-06-17 06:44 UTC] why not https"}],"timestamp":1781678695231}}
{"type":"message","id":"398952be","parentId":"8f07949d","timestamp":"2026-06-17T06:45:02.910Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We **are using HTTPS** for everything! Every single request made by the solver and the publisher went to `https://coinography.com` (using the secure, encrypted protocol).\n\nThe reason you hear the term **\"HTTP\"** used in the logs is because of how the web is structured:\n\n### 1. HTTPS is just \"HTTP\" wearing a seatbelt\n**HTTPS** stands for *Hypertext Transfer Protocol **Secure***. It is not a completely different protocol; it is simply standard **HTTP** traffic wrapped inside an encrypted security tunnel (TLS/SSL). \n\nBecause they use the same underlying protocol:\n* The response codes (like `200 Success`, `201 Created`, or `403 Forbidden`) are still globally referred to as **\"HTTP Status Codes\"**, even when they are delivered securely over **HTTPS**.\n\n### 2. We never use unencrypted HTTP\nIf we were to use standard HTTP (`http://coinography.com`), your WordPress application passwords and your news articles would be sent over the internet in plain text, which is highly insecure. \n\nRest assured, **HTTPS is fully enforced** across this entire setup. Your passwords, draft data, and media uploads are completely encrypted, secure, and private."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gemini-3.5-flash","usage":{"input":94670,"output":657,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":95327,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1781678695250,"responseId":"aUIyatGzAo37mLAPxNeroAw"}}
